A||||20||mbramble||Backfiring...  What Causes It??||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||06-02-2001||05:44 PM||||My 87 DIS Duke has just started backfiring in the past couple of weeks.  I think that's the right time period since my son usually drives it and he doesn't pay as much attention to things as I do!  Anyway, it only happens upon deceleration -- after letting off the gas it will sometimes backfire.  Sometimes very quietly, other times very loud.  Can't seem to put a finger on exactly what circumstances (cold, hot, etc.) that it happens but it is quiet annoying when it happens.  My Check Engine light has not come on but last week I checked the codes just for grins and there was a code set for the oxygen sensor.  I reset the ECM and haven't checked since.  WIll try to do later tonight.  <P>Can anyone give me reasons for backfiring??  Any suggestions or information would be most appreciated.<P>Mike<P>Nope, no codes currently set.....<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||06-02-2001||07:18 PM||||what transmission is in it? Poping out the exhaust... This is real common with decel on a stick. Auto will do it as well under certain conditions. How loud, how much, it is depends on just how fast you are going when you let off the gas. <P>Check all the vac lines and sensor connections just to be safe. <P>Take the MAT out of the intake and inspect it. Replace it if it's been burnt by backfire up the intake or if it's real dirty. This sensor can push the F/A mix rich and contribute to the problem. This sensor won't always set codes when it's messed up. It can also set false codes that point to other things like O2 sensor... <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||06-02-2001||07:23 PM||||It's an auto.  It seems the faster I am going and the faster I slow down, the louder the pop is.  <P>I will check those items.  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrgone||06-02-2001||07:53 PM||||its the exhaust valves sticking open during the power (ignition) stroke isnt it?||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||06-02-2001||08:45 PM||||Here's a theory...  If you got a code for O2 sensor your car may be running rich.  Backfiring is a post exhaust (at the valve) explosion.  I have a V6 auto and it doesn't pop on decel but I'm not sure if it is 'normal' for the 4.  I would think with FI, no motor should pop upon decel unless something is out of whack.  That is because emissions regulations wouldn't allow for post burning of fuel in the exhaust system (not very clean).||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||06-02-2001||10:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Here's a theory...  If you got a code for O2 sensor your car may be running rich.  Backfiring is a post exhaust (at the valve) explosion.  I have a V6 auto and it doesn't pop on decel but I'm not sure if it is 'normal' for the 4.  I would think with FI, no motor should pop upon decel unless something is out of whack.  That is because emissions regulations wouldn't allow for post burning of fuel in the exhaust system (not very clean).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Similar to NASCARs, they run so rich and the exhaust gets so hot, the fuel just explodes in the pipes.  Night races are cool because when they slow down for the turns, you can see some nice fireworks out the bottom of the driver's side.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000006||c m west||06-02-2001||11:30 PM||||exhaust leak or mis-adjusted timing - period<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||06-02-2001||11:32 PM||||Well, I don't think I can do much about the timing since it is a DIS so I will look for an exhaust leak.  Thanks much!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||06-03-2001||03:52 PM||||Timing problem on a DIS motor... not unless the ignition has a major problem....<P>Do Check for cracks in the exhaust manifold. One thing to help prevent cracking... Don't open a wet deck lid with the engine hot. It dumps water right on the manifold and O2 sensor.<P>The manifold has a nasty habit of cracking. I'm on my 3rd one... Also read my torque strut article... It applies to you as well.<P>Still check the MAT sensor. That thing will make the engine run rich if it's screwed up. Just how rich depends in the air temp in the manifold at any given time. ((4 cyl Manifold air temp Is Not the same as ambient air temp. Manifold air can be over 150F even on cool days. All depends on how fast air is moving thru the intake. Manifold air temp can swing 100F in a couple seconds.))||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||06-03-2001||05:32 PM||||Sparkplug Gap!<BR>My 88 DIS 2.5 would backfire and carry on like crazy when the gap got to big. mostly when I accelerated hard. It threw no codes and drove me nuts for about a week. BTW don't use Bosch Platinum plugs they will wear and you cant see the gap change since the electrode will errode down in the porcilen.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000010||theogre||06-04-2001||12:38 PM||||Gap should be 0.060 on the plugs. Make sure all plugs are as close as you can get them. Mismathced gaps can cause trouble just as quick as wrong gap.<P>And like 84 said... Don't use Bosch. I wasn't real impressed with Splitfire on DIS either. The system works just wonderfully with traditional plugs. AC and Autolite both make "Double Platinum" plugs for DIS if you really feel you need them. These plugs have a platinum pad on the "ground" electrode as well as the tip.<P>Since this only happens durring Decel I'm still thinking something is making it run a little rich.||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||06-04-2001||01:54 PM||||Oh and the MAP will also cause backfireing.||||reg||14
Z||000012||mbramble||06-04-2001||06:26 PM||||Checked the spark plugs and all 4 look great and have the proper 0.060 gap.<P>I do have a massive exhaust leak and it appears to be where the exhaust pipe is connected to the manifold.  If I remember from umpteen years ago working on an exhaust system, there is a 'donut' gasket there.  Is that correct??  It looks like a major chore to replace but I guess it needs to be done!!  Whether it has any impact on the backfiring or not.  I thought there was a problem with the muffler as the car got progressively noisier.  I guess this gasket going bad could be a side effect of the badly worn engine/tranny mounts that I replaced a couple months ago....<P>Any tips or gotchas with replacing the donut???<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||06-04-2001||07:07 PM||||It's more annoying than hard. Get under the car and reach up to the bolts with a long extension. A universal joint or wobble tip extension helps. Make sure you use jackstands or large WOOD blocks to support the car. (Never use concrete or brick!)<P>The spring bolts have a nasty habit of breaking or stripping the threads. You can get new ones at most parts shops. They are in the Red MotorMite HELP! packages as well as other places. Take one of the broken ones with you to match it up.||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GTZ34||06-04-2001||09:06 PM||||Actually Eric there are more than 2 reasons for backfire...  I mentioned the one that relates to a condition that MB mentioned in his original post.  Typically, backfire is a pre-existing condition before a tired exhaust system starts come apart but the operator only notices it at that point because the car is much louder and popping is more apparent.  I'm not saying that a broken exhaust 'couldn't' cause backfire but it usually is not the root cause and most often is a symptom.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mbramble||06-05-2001||07:26 AM||||Well, I got the 'donut' replaced with very little problem.  I did go back and buy a new set of spring bolts as the threads on the originals were pretty much rusted away.  Started up the car and not much difference.  As I shined the light down into the engine compartment (it had gotten dark) I could suddenly see the problem -- a crack in the exhaust manifold about an inch from the oxygen sensor.  Guess I'll eventually get around to pulling it and having it welded.....||||reg||1
Z||000016||DecadenceR||06-05-2001||04:17 PM||||When you get that welded make sure you take it to a GOOD shop to do it. Personally I'd just get a new one altogether... But on my 87 Duke (pre-total) my manifold was cracked and the shop welded it. 2 days later, the same thing happened again. Which they welded again. And it again, cracked.<BR>Others in here would know better than i would, but personally I can't see how a repair on something that gets as hot as the exhaust can hold as well as it's originally made to. But that's my opinion. I'm no expert. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||06-05-2001||06:43 PM||||Don't even bother welding it. It's not worth the trouble or cost.<P>Go to the scrap yard and locate any GM FWD car that has the 2.5L motor... Grand Am, Olds Cutlass Calias, others... Those all have the same manifold and they are usually in decent shape. Also usually run only $20-30.<P>If you pull the manifold... Save all the bolts. They usually come out with out too much trouble. It'll give you extras in case you drop one and can't find it.<P>You can also get the manifold new from several places. Dealers and Fiero store have it. I've seen it other places as well.||||reg||1
Z||000018||mbramble||06-05-2001||07:29 PM||||I have a replacement on the way.  My son was out looking at it today with a mirror so he could see better. He called to tell me that it was really bad.  Sure enough, where the runner going up to the passenger side cylinder meets the main tube, it is cracked half way around.  So, as soon as the new one arrives I will pull it off and replace it.....||||reg||1
Z||000019||mbramble||06-05-2001||08:55 PM||||Wow!  On the way home from work today I stopped in at AutoZone and picked up one of those miracle exhaust repair kits.  This one has the roll of cloth that feels like it's impregnated with cement and a bottle of hardener that you mix with water.  I figured what the heck, if I can quiet it down a bit until the new manifold arrives.  I'm taking a trip up into AL this weekend and just didn't want to sound like an old truck any more.  Anyway, applied the stuff and let it dry for two hours per the directions.  It turned rock hard.  Just took the car out and it's amazing!  The first thing I noticed after starting the car and walking around back to have a listen is how much exhaust is now coming out thru the muffler.  Took it for a drive and it's obvious that all the sound is coming out the muffler/tail pipe.  If it justs holds for a week or so I will be happy!  It was a good $2 investment...||||reg||1
Z||000020||mbramble||06-14-2001||12:11 PM||||Well, I got the 'new' manifold that I ordered from Pondski on Tue.  My son and I installed it in about 1 1/2 hours.  Can't believe how smoothly it went.  All bolts broke loose relatively easily.  Everything went back together with no problems.  What a difference!!  I'm sure it's got more umph off the line.  Feels like it anyway.  <P>You should have seen the original manifold.  There was about 1" of the weld left on one of the tubes where it is welded to the main section.  There was also a crack all the way across the main tube right above the O2 sensor.  <P>And, so far (knock on wood) there has been no backfiring since the new manifold was installed.  Taking her on a trip to Albany, GA in the morning for a baseball tourney.  Looking forward to not having to hear all that exhaust noise!!<P>I would say the cause was a combination of the cold water off the decklid and the fact that before I replaced the dogbone and engine and tranny mounts, there was a LOT of engine movement.  I replaced these items several months ago and the strut and the engine mount were trashed.  Tranny mount didn't look bad.  The car has been noisy for quite awhile, maybe since I acquired it from my son.  I'm pretty sure that it has not been as quiet as it is now since I have been driving it.<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
